@@ -0,0 +1,72 @@
+# ECON-02B2d11 — Warehouse salvage and bounded construction supply fulfillment
+
+**Date:** November 1, 2026
+**Project:** Single Galaxy / colony simulation (Linux native development)
+**Exact baseline:** `SingleGalaxy_ECON02B2d10_Source_Recovery_Demand_Source_Tests_2026-11-01.zip`
+**Outcome:** Implemented and tested locally; not merged into GitHub. Windows GUI acceptance remains open.
+
+## Objective and architectural boundary
+
+Turn the previous read-only construction-shortage forecast into *explicitly authorized*, bounded supply jobs, and let existing NPC residents physically reclaim unreserved construction modules from player-built warehouses. Preserve one authoritative simulation writer, material conservation, NPC identity, existing construction reservations, SQL/world restart recovery, and opt-in manufacturing. The warehouse is *not* demolished; salvage means moving **unreserved construction-module stock** out of the warehouse into the central outpost depot.
+
+## Implemented behavior
+
+### 1. Warehouse salvage using a real NPC hauler
+
+- `Action::SalvageConstructionNodeModules` transfers at most the existing worker carrying capacity (two construction modules) from a **built, placed storage node** to the **named existing NPC's carried inventory**. It never mints modules or moves them between distant locations for free.
+- `sg_construction_restock.mode` is migrated with a default of `0` for legacy restock runs and `1` for salvage runs; no existing NPC IDs or jobs are regenerated. A salvage run travels to the node, performs the authoritative pickup, returns to the local depot, and deposits its physical cargo through the already existing resource transition.
+- Only unreserved physical node stock is eligible. In this version a node with outstanding construction-plan reservations is deliberately rejected as a salvage source. The same worker cannot run a construction/restock/salvage job simultaneously, and one active salvage worker is permitted per source node.
+- Cancellation before pickup stops the job without a material movement. Cancellation after pickup switches the NPC to a return-to-depot state; no direct refund or location teleport occurs. Carried inventory, job state, node stock and depot balances persist through SQLite restart.
+- Active salvage sources are excluded from the normal construction source allocator to prevent another project from simultaneously relying on material being removed.
+
+### 2. Explicit, bounded construction supply fulfillment
+
+- `sg_construction_supply_orders` tracks one order for an unfinished authorized structure plan, with owner/ship/site binding, approved amount (1–100 modules), `manufacture` permission, staged count, fabricated count, status and pending authoritative world-command receipt.
+- Creation is an **explicit player/colony-administrator action**. Forecasting alone does not order production. Fabrication is opt-in **off by default**. No new construction personnel are created.
+- Scheduler stages existing **ship construction modules** to the outpost depot before requesting fabrication. If production is permitted, it invokes the existing `FabricateModule` world action, charging **two refined-ore units per construction module**. A maximum approved count bounds the job. Existing equipment, NPC construction, and material-reservation rules are unchanged.
+- If materials or ore are insufficient, the order remains blocked with an actionable shortage reason rather than conjuring inventory.
+- Crash-safe outbox: SQL stores the unique pending command identity, action and quantity before world execution. The worker reconciles it with the world command ID/receipt across restarts; an already committed world operation is never repeated. Cancelling an order prevents *future* commands while an already-pending command must be settled against its actual world outcome.
+- Order counters are advanced only when the authoritative event confirms an applied resource action, and receipt amounts are checked.
+
+### 3. One-authority colony UI and HTTP control
+
+- Added `CREW_SALVAGE`, `CREW_ORDER`, `CREW_ORDER_CANCEL` to the existing native colony protocol and authenticated relay allowlist; owner, site and session checks remain host-side.
+- `viewer/interactive.html` and `viewer/structures.js` include a warehouse salvage button, bounded order quantity, a separate **Allow real-ore fabrication** checkbox (default OFF), order cancellation, and current order progress/status in the colony construction section.
+- No new world-writing browser process, Node.js service, or competing save writer is introduced.
+
+## Testing and evidence
+
+| Test | Result | Details |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Native full project build | PASS | Incremental Linux CMake build completed all targets (initial longer build run timed out during old targets; subsequent run completed successfully) |
+| Non-live CTest regression | **68/68 PASS** | `ctest --output-on-failure -j5 -E 'live\|relay\|HTTP\|http\|native_factory\|integrated'` |
+| Live/relay CTest regression | **29/29 PASS** | Complementary `ctest --output-on-failure -j5 -R 'live\|relay\|HTTP\|http\|native_factory\|integrated'` |
+| JavaScript regression | **93/93 PASS** | `node --test tests/*.js` |
+| Offline Chromium UI | **2/2 PASS** | `python scripts/colony_crew_browser_smoke.py` and `python scripts/colony_structures_browser_smoke.py` |
+| New salvage dedicated native test | PASS | Reserve-conflict rejection, physical pickup from built node, mid-haul restart, delivery, cancellation, inventory/mass conservation |
+| New supply order dedicated native test | PASS | Validated scope, quantity cap, real-ore fabrication, post-world/pre-SQL restart, receipt reconciliation exactly once, conservation |
+| Live HTTP integration | PASS | Forged-token rejection, invalid-node rejection, genuine NPC warehouse-to-depot salvage, bounded supply-order acceptance/cancellation |
+
+All 97 native tests passed in **two complementary groups**, not as one uninterrupted command. The project-wide JavaScript test count is 93; the separate `.cjs` files require their own native-test environment variables and are handled by the native/CTest suites. A naive `node --test tests/*.js tests/*.cjs` without the CTest harness fails on missing executable environment variables, not on an application regression. Do not misinterpret that invocation.
+
+## Files touched
+
+- `include/galaxy/Simulation.hpp`, `src/Simulation.cpp`, `src/Persistence.cpp`: new authoritative salvage action, snapshot v34 and persisted resource receipts.
+- `include/galaxy/NpcConstructionCrews.hpp`, `src/NpcConstructionCrews.cpp`: durable salvage mode, approved supply order, material movement and recovery outbox.
+- `src/factory_colony_protocol.hpp`, `live/interactive_relay.py`: authenticated operations and validated routing.
+- `viewer/interactive.html`, `viewer/structures.js`: authorized salvage/fulfillment controls and live job balances.
+- `tests/ECON02B2d11SalvageTests.cpp`, `tests/ECON02B2d11SupplyOrderTests.cpp`, `tests/ECON02B2d6ConstructionCrewHostTests.py`, `scripts/colony_crew_browser_smoke.py`, `CMakeLists.txt`: tests, routes and build integration.
+
+## Known limitations and later gates
+
+- **Warehouse salvage is not demolition.** Structure, fittings, and other goods remain. Safe decommission/demolition requires independent ownership, inventory-empty, worker-safety and reservation checks.
+- Construction module transport is still a provisional **local 2D grid** with a two-module NPC capacity; true 3D navigation and distant logistics remain future work.
+- Fulfillment only stages modules from the colony ship, optionally fabricating them from its actual refined ore; it is not yet a complete arbitrary-factory production planner, industrial logistics network, or automatic inventory purchasing system.
+- Colony managed absence still requires a broader NPC economy, infrastructure upkeep and multi-resource production chain. No unrestricted background job or autonomous consent to manufacture has been granted.
+- The full Windows native/GUI acceptance gates have not been exercised in this Linux environment.
+
+## Reproducibility and repository policy
+
+The source ZIP contains a complete updated `single-galaxy/` snapshot. The `.diff` is generated against the **exact d10 archived source**. Applying the patch to that baseline must reproduce the new source **byte-for-byte**, and `git diff --check` must pass. The SHA-256 manifest lists all deliverables. Development and tests were performed locally; GitHub is repository storage/verification only, and no GitHub mutation was made in this milestone.
+
+**Suggested next milestone:** ECON-02B2d12 safe warehouse decommissioning with residue accounting, no player inactivity penalty, and multi-resource hauling protection. Alternatively a scoped chain-of-production planner for shortages could be prioritized before demolition.
